WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:06.800
Universal's 1999 remake of "The Mummy"&nbsp;
was a cultural reset for both the property&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:13.280
and Hollywood as a whole. It arguably changed&nbsp;
action movies, helping usher in the era of CGI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.960
villains and heavily comedic heroes. One of&nbsp;
those heroes, Rachel Weisz's Evelyn Carnahan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:24.320
propels the action of the first "Mummy" film.&nbsp;
She is the one who recognizes the significance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:30.880
of the puzzle box that leads them to the city of&nbsp;
Hamunaptra. She reads the incantation that awakens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:37.120
Imhotep, and it is her soul that the undead priest&nbsp;
wants to give his dearly departed girlfriend.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.640
Evelyn is a huge part of the&nbsp;
"Mummy" franchise's appeal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.560
Fans love the movie's blend of action, romance,&nbsp;
and comedy — all of which Weisz contributes to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.440
She channels screwball comediennes of&nbsp;
the '30s for her sparkling performance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:56.720
The Stranger noted in its review how&nbsp;
perfect she was to play Evelyn, saying,

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:01.040
"[She has] all the spunk and ditzy charm&nbsp;
you'd expect from a heroine who can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:04.800
proudly proclaim she's not&nbsp;
just smart. She's a librarian!"

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:13.680
The "Mummy" series arguably fell off when&nbsp;
Weisz failed to return for "The Mummy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.280
Tomb of the Dragon Emperor," the third installment&nbsp;
in the "Mummy" series. A representative of Weisz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:24.240
told Digital Spy that she didn't want to go&nbsp;
to China and spend so much time away from her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.400
at the time, newborn son. After&nbsp;
leaving the "Mummy" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:31.840
Weisz went on to work steadily&nbsp;
in movies both big and small.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:37.280
But her most critically acclaimed work of late&nbsp;
has been on a much smaller scale than "The Mummy."

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.520
Rachel Weisz won an Academy Award not&nbsp;
long after "The Mummy Returns" for her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:47.200
performance in the 2005 adaptation of&nbsp;
John Le Carré's "The Constant Gardener."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.960
The film centers on Ralph Fiennes' Justin Quayle,&nbsp;
who tries to solve the murder of his wife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:58.160
Weisz plays the ill-fated wife,&nbsp;
Tessa, an Amnesty activist in Kenya.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.360
Weisz also has had some brief dalliances with&nbsp;
other blockbuster franchises post-"Mummy." The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.480
same year she starred in "The Constant Gardener,"&nbsp;
she made a foray into comic book adaptations with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.360
the cult favorite "Constantine." She'd go&nbsp;
on to star in "The Bourne Legacy," opposite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:20.160
Jeremy Renner, before doing her time in the MCU,&nbsp;
playing Melina Vostokoff in "Black Widow" in 2021.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.960
Vostokoff is something of a mother figure&nbsp;
to Scarlett Johansson's Natasha Romanoff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:30.160
and Florence Pugh's Yelena Belova. As of&nbsp;
yet, there's been no word if Vostokoff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.640
will return to the MCU, but there's little&nbsp;
doubt fans would adore seeing her again.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.920
While Wiesz's experience with&nbsp;
blockbusters is extensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.680
her most notable work of late&nbsp;
has been in more indie fare.

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:48.160
Weisz first worked with Greek director Yorgos&nbsp;
Lanthimos on his 2015 black comedy "The Lobster."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:53.360
She played a woman searching for love before she&nbsp;
gets turned into an animal. She would reunite with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.680
Lanthimos for 2018's "The Favourite," in which she&nbsp;
plays Lady Sarah Churchill. Lady Sarah starts the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:05.360
film as the titular "favourite" of Queen Anne,&nbsp;
played by Olivia Colman, as well as her secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:11.680
girlfriend. Emma Stone plays Abigail, who woos the&nbsp;
Queen away from Lady Sarah in an attempt to stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:17.440
in power at court. Colman won an Academy Award&nbsp;
for her performance as Queen Anne, the apex of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:22.800
the unlikely love triangle, while Stone and Weisz&nbsp;
were both nominated for Best Supporting Actress.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.560
The same year as "The Favourite,"&nbsp;
Weisz starred in "Disobedience,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.680
opposite Rachel McAdams. The drama&nbsp;
focuses on two women from the Orthodox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:37.600
Jewish community who try to reconcile their love&nbsp;
for each other with their sometimes overbearingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:44.560
strict community. Despite being married to a&nbsp;
man—Daniel Craig, a.k.a. James Bond— Weisz's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:51.680
performances in these films have vaulted Weisz&nbsp;
into queer icon status in the LGBTQ community.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.560
Weisz's queer turn may have something to do&nbsp;
with the fact that she's primarily focused&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:59.920
on women-centered stories.&nbsp;
She told the New York Times,

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.640
"I really enjoy all the thousands of&nbsp;
movies I've seen about men [...] I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.560
there are some great masterpieces.&nbsp;
But there's just a dearth of ones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.440
about women. I love women. Women are just&nbsp;
really fascinating and different to men."

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:17.920
Fans can likely expect this trend to&nbsp;
continue with Weisz's future projects.

